# libcss: the selector hash's element, class and id tables grow (patch 501)
# Generated 2026-11-28
# Base: seleron/vitabrowser (patch stack through 500)
#
# Every stylesheet's selector hash had DEFAULT_SLOTS (64) chains per table
# for its whole life. A sheet with thousands of class selectors -- the
# simulator saw class tables past 8,000 entries on stackoverflow.com and
# rte.ie, past 2,000 on github, oldreddit and youtube -- had chains of
# dozens to over a hundred, and every class of every element walked one
# to its first match and on (css__selector_hash_find_by_class,
# _iterate_classes: ~7% of stackoverflow's page-load main-thread time).
#
# Each table now counts its entries and, past two per chain, is rebuilt
# with four times the chains (up to 65,536). Entries move WITH their
# stored bloom -- libcss's head removal leaves the removed selector's
# bloom on the chain head, and regenerating it would change that -- and
# are placed by _insert_into_chain's own comparison (ascending specificity,
# then rule index, equal keys after), walking each old chain in order: the
# entries of one name are all in one old chain and all go to one new
# chain, so their order -- all find and iterate depend on -- is the same.
# The nodes a rebuild may need are allocated first; out of memory, the
# table stays as it is. Matches handed out by find / iterate live only for
# one selection, and inserts never happen during one.
#
# Verified before landing: a VITA_HASH_CHECK build compared, after each
# rebuild, every name's (selector, bloom) sequence before and after --
# 196 rebuilds over the 21 census sites and the selector / CSSOM / cascade
# test pages, 0 mismatches. The check code stays behind that flag.
